The Rise of Populism and Rejection of Globalisation
Over the past two decades, populist movements have risen in many countries, opposing
globalisation and championing nationalism. Leaders like Donald Trump, Viktor Orbán, and
movements like Brexit have challenged global trade agreements, migration policies, and
international institutions. These movements criticise globalisation for eroding national
sovereignty, exacerbating economic inequality, and threatening national identities. The US-China
trade war and Brexit reflect a broader trend of states retreating from global integration.
Realist Explanation Liberal Explanation
Realists interpret the rise of populism and the
rejection of globalisation as a reaffirmation of
their belief in the primacy of state sovereignty.
For realists, globalisation has not diminished
the fundamental anarchic nature of the
international system, and the populist backlash
underscores the enduring importance of the
nation-state. Populist policies, such as
“America First” or Brexit, reflect states
prioritising their national interests and
asserting their sovereignty against perceived
external threats, such as immigration or
economic dependence on foreign powers.
Realists also view this backlash as evidence of
the limitations of international institutions and
agreements. From a realist perspective, these
institutions are tools for powerful states to
advance their own interests, and when states
feel these tools no longer serve them, they will
abandon them. For example, the UK’s
withdrawal from the European Union illustrates
a realist belief that states will reject
cooperative frameworks when they conflict
with national interests. Similarly, the US-China
trade war reflects realist assumptions about
states using economic tools as weapons in a
competitive international system.
Liberals explain the rise of populism as a
reaction to the unequal distribution of
globalisation’s benefits rather than a
fundamental rejection of interconnectedness.
They argue that while globalisation has
created significant economic growth, it has
also left behind certain groups, leading to
dissatisfaction and populist backlash. Liberals
suggest that this rejection of globalisation is
not inevitable but a response to policy failures,
such as insufficient redistribution or inadequate
protections for vulnerable workers.
Liberals also highlight the danger populism
poses to the rules-based international order
they champion. Populist leaders often reject
international institutions and agreements,
undermining the frameworks that liberals see
as essential for managing global challenges.
However, liberals remain optimistic that
reforms to globalisation—such as fairer trade
policies or enhanced support for those harmed
by economic integration—can address the root
causes of populism and renew trust in
international cooperation.
