Global Governance: Political and Economic
The Failure of the United Nations Security Council to Prevent War
The United Nations Security Council (UNSC), established to maintain international peace and
security, has faced significant criticism for its inability to prevent or resolve major conflicts,
including the wars in Ukraine, Gaza, and the protracted civil war in Syria. The Syrian conflict, in
particular, persisted for over a decade, resulting in hundreds of thousands of deaths and millions
displaced. The UNSC has been paralysed by the competing interests of its permanent members
(P5), such as Russia and the United States, which have vetoed resolutions related to ceasefires,
humanitarian aid, and accountability for war crimes. Similarly, the UNSC has been ineffective in
addressing the war in Ukraine due to Russia’s vetoes and has failed to deliver decisive action in
the Israel-Hamas conflict due to deep geopolitical divides. These failures have exposed the
limitations of the UNSC in dealing with contemporary security challenges.
