The Decreased Effectiveness of the World Trade Organisation (WTO)
The World Trade Organization (WTO), established to promote free trade and resolve disputes,
has faced growing criticism for its decreased effectiveness. The WTO’s dispute resolution
mechanism has been paralysed since 2019 due to the United States blocking appointments to
the appellate body, whilst an increasing number of states are ignoring WTO rules. Additionally,
multilateral trade agreements have stagnated, with states increasingly turning to bilateral or
regional trade deals, undermining the WTO’s relevance.
Realist Explanation Liberal Explanation
Realists view the decline of the WTO as
evidence that states remain the dominant
actors in global politics and that international
institutions are only effective as long as they
align with state interests. From a realist
perspective, the paralysis of the WTO’s
appellate body reflects the prioritisation of
national sovereignty over global trade rules, as
seen in the United States’ decision to block
reforms to protect its economic interests. For
realists, this is a natural outcome of the
anarchic international system, where states
use institutions as tools to advance their power
but withdraw or obstruct them when they no
longer serve their interests.
Realists argue that the stagnation of
multilateral agreements reflects the
competitive nature of global politics, where
states prefer bilateral and regional deals that
offer greater control and flexibility. The rise of
trade conflicts, such as the US-China trade
war, further demonstrates realist concerns
about how economic interconnectedness
becomes a site of power struggles rather than
cooperation.
Liberals analyse the decreased effectiveness
of the WTO as a concerning development that
undermines the principles of free trade and
multilateralism. They argue that the WTO’s
challenges stem from the failure of states to
commit fully to the institution and adapt it to
contemporary challenges. For liberals, the
paralysis of the dispute resolution mechanism
is a symptom of broader geopolitical tensions
that must be addressed through dialogue and
reform.
Despite these challenges, liberals emphasise
the continued importance of the WTO in
promoting a rules-based international trade
system and note its success in expanding free
trade significantly since its founding. They
advocate for efforts to revitalise the institution,
such as appointing appellate judges,
modernising trade rules to reflect technological
and environmental issues, and fostering
greater cooperation among member states.
Liberals see the WTO’s struggles not as proof
of its irrelevance but as an urgent call for
reform to preserve the benefits of global trade
and interdependence.
The Success/Failure of International Governmental Institutions in Tackling Poverty
