The Paris Climate Agreement
Adopted in 2015, the Paris Climate Agreement is a landmark global accord aiming to limit global
warming to well below 2°C above pre-industrial levels, with efforts to limit it to 1.5°C. The
agreement is based on voluntary national contributions (Nationally Determined Contributions, or
NDCs) and emphasises global cooperation to combat climate change. Despite its ambitious
goals, the agreement has faced challenges, including insufficient commitments, slow
implementation, and the withdrawal and re-entry of major powers like the United States. The
ongoing rise in global emissions and the difficulty in holding countries accountable for their
targets have raised questions about the effectiveness of the agreement.
