The Current Power of the United States
The United States remains a dominant global power, with unmatched military capabilities, a
leading position in global financial systems, and significant cultural and technological influence.
However, its relative power has declined compared to its post-Cold War peak, as rising powers
like China and regional players like India and the EU assert themselves on the global stage.
Domestic political divisions, shifting alliances, and challenges to its global leadership (e.g.,
withdrawal from international agreements) have also raised questions about the sustainability of
U.S. dominance.
Realist Analysis Liberal Explanation
Realists explain the United States’ position as
consistent with their view of great power
dynamics in an anarchic system. While the
U.S. remains the preeminent global power,
realists argue that its relative decline is
inevitable due to the balancing efforts of rising
powers like China and Russia. This reflects the
realist principle that states seek to
counterbalance dominant powers to prevent
hegemony.
Realists also emphasise the importance of
hard power in maintaining U.S. influence. They
argue that its military dominance, as
demonstrated through alliances like NATO and
its global network of bases, remains the
