Regionalism And The EU
The Spread and Effectiveness of Economic Regionalism
Economic regionalism, the creation of regional blocs to promote trade and economic integration,
has grown significantly over the past few decades. Examples include the Association of
Southeast Asian Nations (ASEAN), the African Continental Free Trade Area (AfCFTA), and the
Comprehensive and Progressive Agreement for Trans-Pacific Partnership (CPTPP). These
agreements aim to reduce trade barriers, foster economic cooperation, and enhance regional
stability and have been highly successful in promoting regional trade.
Realist Explanation Liberal Explanation
Realists view the spread of economic
regionalism as a pragmatic tool used by states
to maximise their economic power and
compete in a globalised world. According to
realists, regional blocs are not created out of a
desire for collective progress but as a means
for states to secure their national interests,
such as enhancing their economic
competitiveness or balancing against external
powers. For instance, the CPTPP can be seen
as a counterweight to China’s growing
economic influence in the Asia-Pacific region.
Realists also highlight the limits of economic
regionalism, as states remain reluctant to cede
significant sovereignty to regional institutions.
For example, ASEAN operates primarily on a
consensus-based model, reflecting the realist
view that states prioritise sovereignty over
deeper integration. From a realist perspective,
economic regionalism is an extension of state
competition, where states cooperate only to
the extent that it serves their strategic
interests.
Liberals view the spread of economic
regionalism as a positive development that
fosters interdependence, reduces the
likelihood of conflict, and promotes shared
prosperity. They argue that regional economic
integration creates incentives for cooperation
and dialogue, as states become increasingly
reliant on one another for trade and
investment. For example, the African
Continental Free Trade Area (AfCFTA) is
expected to boost intra-African trade and
contribute to economic development, aligning
with liberal ideals of collective progress.
Liberals also emphasise the role of regional
organisations in creating norms, institutions,
and frameworks that facilitate cooperation.
They see economic regionalism as a stepping
stone toward global integration, as successful
regional blocs can act as models for broader
multilateral agreements.
The Widening and Deepening of the European Union
https://www.politicsexplained.co.uk/ 12
Since 2000, the European Union (EU) has significantly expanded its membership and deepened
its integration through key treaties and institutional reforms. The EU welcomed 13 new member
states between 2004 and 2013, primarily from Central and Eastern Europe, in one of the most
ambitious enlargements in its history. This widening was driven by a desire to promote stability,
democracy, and economic growth in the post-Cold War era. Simultaneously, the EU deepened
its integration with the Lisbon Treaty (2007), which streamlined decision-making processes,
expanded the powers of the European Parliament, and established new roles such as the
President of the European Council and High Representative for Foreign Affairs. These
developments sought to enhance the EU’s capacity to function effectively as a bloc while
accommodating a larger and more diverse membership.
Realist Explanation Liberal Explanation
Realists interpret the EU’s post-2000
developments as driven by pragmatic state
interests rather than idealistic notions of unity
or shared values. They argue that the EU’s
enlargement into Central and Eastern Europe
was primarily a strategy to stabilise the region
and counter Russian influence. By integrating
countries like Poland, Hungary, and the Baltic
states, the EU extended its geopolitical reach
and created a buffer zone against potential
Russian aggression, aligning with realist
concerns about balancing power.
Realists also highlight the limits of deepening
integration, as member states remain reluctant
to cede significant sovereignty. The Lisbon
Treaty, while centralising some aspects of EU
governance, was a compromise designed to
address member states’ concerns about
retaining control over key areas like foreign
policy and taxation. Realists point to continued
disagreements among member states—such
as differing views on migration, fiscal policy,
and security—as evidence that national
interests constrain the EU’s deepening.
Liberals view the EU’s widening and
deepening since 2000 as a triumph of
multilateralism and regional integration. They
argue that the inclusion of Central and Eastern
European countries reflects the EU’s
commitment to promoting democracy, human
rights, and economic development in formerly
unstable regions. The enlargement process,
with its strict accession criteria, is seen as a
liberal success story, fostering peace and
prosperity in areas that were once sources of
conflict.
The Lisbon Treaty is similarly celebrated by
liberals as a milestone in enhancing the EU’s
ability to act cohesively on the global stage. By
streamlining decision-making and
strengthening institutions like the European
Parliament, the treaty reinforced the EU’s
democratic legitimacy and capacity to address
collective challenges. Liberals view these
developments as evidence of the EU’s role as
a model for regional integration, demonstrating
the importance of supranational organisations
and how shared sovereignty can achieve
greater stability and prosperity in a globalised
world.
The Reluctance of Regional Organisations Other Than the EU to Pursue
Supranationalism
While the European Union has embraced supranationalism, with member states ceding
significant sovereignty to central institutions, other regional organisations have been more
reluctant to adopt this model. ASEAN, the African Union (AU), and the Arab League are
intergovernmental rather than supranational institutions and there has been little move towards
further political intergreation. This reluctance is often attributed to concerns over sovereignty,
economic disparities, and diverse political systems among member states.
